## The core idea
JavaScript on the page runs on **one main thread**, which it shares with style, layout, paint and input handling. The **event loop** is the scheduler that decides what runs next. Everything async in the browser is just "put a callback in some queue, and the loop will run it later". Knowing which queue a callback lands in lets you predict output order and explain why a page freezes.

## The pieces
- **Call stack**: the functions currently executing. The loop only picks new work when the stack is empty.
- **Task queues** (often called macrotasks): `setTimeout`/`setInterval` callbacks, DOM events (click, input), `MessageChannel`/`postMessage`, network callbacks from XHR, the initial script run. Browsers keep several task queues and may prioritise input over timers.
- **Microtask queue**: promise reactions (`.then/.catch/.finally`), continuations after `await`, `queueMicrotask`, `MutationObserver` callbacks.
- **Rendering steps**: `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks, then style, layout, paint. These run when the browser decides it is time for a frame, typically about every 16.7 ms on a 60 Hz display, and only if something needs updating.

## One turn of the loop
```text
+--> pick ONE task from a task queue, run it to completion
|        |
|        v
|    drain the microtask queue (including microtasks added while draining)
|        |
|        v
|    is it time to render?  -- no --+
|        | yes                      |
|        v                          |
|    run rAF callbacks              |
|    style -> layout -> paint       |
|        |                          |
+--------+--------------------------+
     (idle time? run requestIdleCallback callbacks)
```

Three rules explain almost every puzzle:

1. Synchronous code runs to completion first.
2. After each task, **all** microtasks run before anything else, including rendering.
3. Each timer or event callback is its own task, so microtasks run between two timeouts.

## Output-order puzzles
```ts
console.log("A");
setTimeout(() => console.log("B"), 0);
Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => console.log("C"))
  .then(() => console.log("D"));
queueMicrotask(() => console.log("E"));
console.log("F");
// A F C E D B
```

`C` and `E` were queued during the script; `D` is queued only when `C`'s callback finishes, so it lands after `E`. The timeout is a task and waits for the microtask queue to empty.

### async/await
`await x` is roughly `Promise.resolve(x).then(rest of function)`. Code before the first `await` runs synchronously.

```ts
async function run() {
  console.log("1");
  await null;
  console.log("2");
}
console.log("3");
run();
console.log("4");
// 3 1 4 2
```

### Timeouts and microtasks interleave
```ts
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("t1");
  Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("p1"));
}, 0);
setTimeout(() => console.log("t2"), 0);
// t1 p1 t2  (microtasks drain after each task)
```

## requestAnimationFrame and requestIdleCallback
`requestAnimationFrame(cb)` runs `cb` right before the next paint, after tasks and microtasks of that turn. Use it for visual updates and for batching DOM reads and writes so they line up with frames. It is paused in background tabs. A `setTimeout(fn, 16)` animation drifts and can run twice per frame or skip frames; rAF does not.

`requestIdleCallback(cb, { timeout })` runs `cb` when the browser has idle time at the end of a frame, with a deadline object telling you how much time is left. Use it for low-priority work such as prefetching or analytics batching. Note that Safari does not ship `requestIdleCallback` by default, so feature-detect and fall back to `setTimeout`.

## Long tasks and INP
Any task longer than **50 ms** is a long task. While it runs, clicks and keypresses wait, which directly hurts **Interaction to Next Paint**. Fixes:

```ts
async function processAll(items: Item[]) {
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    process(items[i]);
    if (i % 100 === 0) await yieldToMain();
  }
}

function yieldToMain(): Promise<void> {
  // scheduler.yield() keeps priority and resumes before other queued tasks where supported
  const s = (globalThis as { scheduler?: { yield?: () => Promise<void> } }).scheduler;
  if (s?.yield) return s.yield();
  return new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 0));
}
```

Yielding with a promise (`await Promise.resolve()`) does **not** help: it is a microtask, so the browser still cannot render or handle input. You must yield to a task. For genuinely heavy CPU work (parsing a big CSV, image processing) move it to a **Web Worker**, which has its own thread and event loop.

## Node versus the browser
Node uses libuv and runs the loop in **phases**: timers, pending callbacks, poll (I/O), check (`setImmediate`), close callbacks. Differences that come up:

- `process.nextTick` callbacks run before promise microtasks, after the current operation. Recursive `nextTick` can starve I/O.
- `setImmediate` runs in the check phase. Inside an I/O callback, `setImmediate` always fires before `setTimeout(fn, 0)`; in the main module the order between them is not guaranteed.
- No rendering steps and no rAF.

```ts
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("promise"));
process.nextTick(() => console.log("nextTick"));
// nextTick promise
```

## Common mistakes
- Saying `setTimeout(fn, 0)` runs "immediately". It runs after the current task, all microtasks and possibly a render, and browsers clamp nested timers to at least 4 ms.
- Believing promises run on another thread. They are just a different queue on the same thread.
- Chunking work with `await Promise.resolve()` and wondering why the UI is still frozen.
- Forgetting that code before the first `await` is synchronous.

## In the interview
Expect an output-order snippet. Talk through it out loud: "synchronous first, then the microtask queue drains, then the next task". Name which callbacks are microtasks. Then connect it to real impact: long tasks block input and hurt INP, microtask loops starve rendering, and the fix is yielding to a task or moving work to a worker. Bonus points for knowing rAF runs before paint, `scheduler.yield()`, and the Node `nextTick` ordering.

## The core idea
CSS bugs in interviews and in production usually come from three mechanisms: **the cascade** (which declaration wins), **formatting contexts** (how boxes are sized and placed) and **stacking contexts** (what paints on top). Modern CSS (layers, grid, container queries, logical properties) removes most of the old hacks if you know when to use each.

## The cascade
When several declarations set the same property on the same element, the browser compares them in this order and stops at the first difference:

1. **Origin and importance**: user-agent, user, author styles; `!important` reverses the order.
2. **Inline style** attribute (beats any selector).
3. **Cascade layers** (`@layer`): later layers beat earlier ones; unlayered styles beat all layers.
4. **Specificity** of the selector.
5. **Source order**: the last one wins.

### Specificity
Specificity is a triple `(A, B, C)` compared left to right:

- **A**: id selectors (`#id`).
- **B**: classes, attribute selectors and pseudo-classes (`.btn`, `[type=text]`, `:hover`).
- **C**: type selectors and pseudo-elements (`a`, `::before`).

```css
a                     /* (0,0,1) */
.nav a                /* (0,1,1) */
.nav .item a:hover    /* (0,3,1) */
#menu a               /* (1,0,1)  beats any number of classes */
:is(#menu, .nav) a    /* (1,0,1)  :is() takes its most specific argument */
:where(#menu) a       /* (0,0,1)  :where() always adds zero */
```

The universal selector `*` and combinators add nothing. `:not()` and `:has()` count as their most specific argument.

### Cascade layers
Layers let you decide precedence by **intent** instead of specificity wars:

```css
@layer reset, base, components, utilities;

@layer components {
  .card .title { color: var(--fg); }
}
@layer utilities {
  .text-red { color: red; }  /* wins over the components rule despite lower specificity */
}
```

This is how modern design systems and Tailwind v4 order resets, base styles, components and utilities. Third-party CSS can be imported into a low layer with `@import url(lib.css) layer(vendor);`.

## The box model
Every box has content, padding, border and margin. With the default `box-sizing: content-box`, `width` sets only the content width, so padding and border add to it. Almost every codebase sets:

```css
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
```

so `width` includes padding and border. Vertical margins between blocks **collapse** (the larger wins) in normal flow, but not inside flex or grid containers.

## Flexbox versus grid
**Flexbox** distributes space along **one axis**, and items size from their content. **Grid** defines **rows and columns** on the container, and items are placed into them.

```css
/* Flexbox: a toolbar, items sized by content, one pushed to the end */
.toolbar { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; }
.toolbar .spacer { margin-inline-start: auto; }

/* Grid: responsive card layout with no media queries */
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
}

/* Grid: page shell with named areas */
.shell {
  display: grid;
  grid-template: "header header" auto "nav main" 1fr / 240px 1fr;
  min-height: 100dvh;
}
```

Use flexbox for nav bars, button groups, centring and anything where content decides size. Use grid for page layouts, card galleries, forms with aligned labels, and anything that must line up in two directions. `subgrid` lets nested items align to the parent grid tracks, which fixes the "card titles of different heights" problem.

A classic flexbox gotcha: flex items have `min-width: auto`, so long text or a wide child refuses to shrink and overflows. Fix with `min-width: 0` on the item.

## Positioning and stacking contexts
`position: relative` offsets an element from its normal spot; `absolute` positions relative to the nearest positioned ancestor; `fixed` to the viewport (unless an ancestor has a `transform`, which becomes the containing block); `sticky` behaves like relative until a scroll threshold.

`z-index` only orders elements **within the same stacking context**. A new stacking context is created by, among others: positioned elements with a `z-index` other than `auto`, `opacity` below 1, `transform`, `filter`, `will-change` of those, `isolation: isolate`, and flex or grid children with a `z-index`.

```text
root stacking context
 +-- header (z-index: 10)
 +-- main (transform: translateZ(0))   <- creates a context, effective z = auto/0
      +-- modal (z-index: 1000)        <- 1000 only within main
Result: header paints above the modal.
```

Fixes: render modals and toasts in a portal at the end of `body`, or use the native `<dialog>` with `showModal()` and the popover API, which put the element in the browser's **top layer** above all stacking contexts.

## Container queries and responsive design
Media queries ask about the viewport. Container queries ask about the component's container, so the same card can be horizontal in a wide main column and stacked in a narrow sidebar.

```css
.card-wrap { container-type: inline-size; }

@container (min-width: 480px) {
  .card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr; }
}
```

Other modern responsive tools: `clamp()` for fluid type (`font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.9rem + 0.5vw, 1.25rem)`), `dvh`/`svh` units that handle mobile browser toolbars, and `aspect-ratio` to reserve space for media.

## Logical properties
`margin-inline-start` instead of `margin-left`, `padding-block` instead of top and bottom, `inset-inline-end` instead of `right`. They follow the writing direction, so an RTL language layout works without a separate stylesheet.

## CSS architecture at scale
| Approach | Strengths | Costs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CSS Modules | Locally scoped class names, plain CSS, zero runtime | Theming and variants need conventions; styles live in a separate file |
| Utility-first (Tailwind) | No naming, tiny final CSS, consistent design tokens, fast to change | Long class lists; needs component abstraction for reuse |
| Runtime CSS-in-JS (styled-components, Emotion) | Co-location, dynamic props | Runtime cost on every render, harder with React Server Components and streaming |
| Zero-runtime CSS-in-JS (vanilla-extract, Panda, StyleX) | Type-safe, extracted at build time | Build tooling complexity |

The industry trend since React Server Components is away from runtime CSS-in-JS toward CSS Modules, Tailwind or build-time extraction, with design tokens as CSS custom properties.

## Common mistakes
- Fixing specificity problems with `!important` or more ids instead of layers or flatter selectors.
- Raising `z-index` without checking for a stacking context on an ancestor.
- Using grid for a simple row of buttons, or flexbox with percentage widths for a real two-dimensional layout.
- Forgetting `min-width: 0` on flex children that contain long text.

## In the interview
They will give you a specificity puzzle or a z-index bug. Compute specificity as a triple out loud and mention that layers are compared before specificity. For z-index, say "stacking context" in the first sentence and list what creates one. For flexbox versus grid, give the one-dimension versus two-dimension rule plus a concrete example of each. For architecture, compare by runtime cost, scoping, theming and server component compatibility rather than by taste.

## The core idea
TypeScript in UI code is not about annotating everything. It is about making **wrong states unrepresentable**, keeping types flowing from one source of truth (an API schema, a design token object) instead of duplicating them, and stopping at the boundary where runtime data enters, because types do not exist at runtime.

## Typing props and events
```tsx
import type { ComponentPropsWithoutRef, ReactNode, ChangeEvent } from "react";

type ButtonProps = ComponentPropsWithoutRef<"button"> & {
  variant?: "primary" | "ghost";
  icon?: ReactNode;
};

export function Button({ variant = "primary", icon, children, ...rest }: ButtonProps) {
  return <button data-variant={variant} {...rest}>{icon}{children}</button>;
}

function Search({ onSearch }: { onSearch: (q: string) => void }) {
  const onChange = (e: ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => onSearch(e.target.value);
  return <input type="search" onChange={onChange} />;
}
```

`ComponentPropsWithoutRef<"button">` gives you every native attribute (`disabled`, `aria-*`, `onClick`) so wrappers stay accessible. In React 19, `ref` is a normal prop for function components, so `ComponentProps<"button">` including `ref` also works without `forwardRef`. Type children as `ReactNode`. Prefer named callbacks in props (`onSelect(id: string)`) over passing raw events up.

## Discriminated unions for state
The common anti-pattern is a bag of optional flags:

```ts
// Allows nonsense: isLoading true AND error set AND data present
type Bad = { isLoading: boolean; error?: string; data?: User[] };
```

A discriminated union lists the states that can actually exist:

```ts
type Remote<T> =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: T }
  | { status: "error"; error: Error };

function UserList({ state }: { state: Remote<User[]> }) {
  switch (state.status) {
    case "idle":    return null;
    case "loading": return <Spinner />;
    case "success": return <ul>{state.data.map((u) => <li key={u.id}>{u.name}</li>)}</ul>;
    case "error":   return <ErrorBox error={state.error} />;
    default:        return assertNever(state);
  }
}

function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled state: ${JSON.stringify(x)}`);
}
```

If someone adds `{ status: "stale" }`, every switch without that case fails to compile. The same pattern models reducer actions, form steps and WebSocket messages.

## Narrowing
TypeScript narrows a union inside control flow using:
- `typeof x === "string"`, `Array.isArray(x)`, `x instanceof Date`
- `"data" in x` (property presence)
- equality on a discriminant (`x.status === "success"`)
- truthiness (`if (user)`) for `null`/`undefined`
- custom **type predicates**:

```ts
function isDefined<T>(x: T | null | undefined): x is T {
  return x != null;
}
const ids = maybeIds.filter(isDefined); // string[] not (string | null)[]
```

Since TypeScript 5.5, simple filters like `arr.filter((x) => x !== null)` infer the predicate automatically.

## Generics for components and hooks
Generics keep the caller's type instead of collapsing to `any` or a base type.

```tsx
type ListProps<T> = {
  items: T[];
  getKey: (item: T) => string;
  render: (item: T) => ReactNode;
};

export function List<T>({ items, getKey, render }: ListProps<T>) {
  return <ul>{items.map((i) => <li key={getKey(i)}>{render(i)}</li>)}</ul>;
}

// T is inferred as Product; render gets a typed product
<List items={products} getKey={(p) => p.sku} render={(p) => p.title} />;
```

```ts
function useLocalStorage<T>(key: string, initial: T) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState<T>(() => {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(key);
    return raw === null ? initial : (JSON.parse(raw) as T); // still unchecked: validate if it matters
  });
  useEffect(() => localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value)), [key, value]);
  return [value, setValue] as const; // tuple, not (T | Dispatch)[]
}
```

Use constraints when you need properties: `<T extends { id: string }>`.

## Utility types you should know
| Utility | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `Partial<T>`, `Required<T>` | form drafts, patch payloads |
| `Pick<T, K>`, `Omit<T, K>` | derive view models from entities |
| `Record<K, V>` | lookup maps keyed by a union |
| `ReturnType<typeof fn>`, `Awaited<T>` | type from a function or promise |
| `NonNullable<T>` | strip `null` and `undefined` |
| `Extract<U, X>`, `Exclude<U, X>` | filter union members |
| `ComponentProps<typeof Comp>` | reuse another component's props |

## satisfies
`satisfies` checks that a value matches a type **without changing the value's inferred type**.

```ts
type Theme = Record<"primary" | "danger", { bg: string; fg: string }>;

const theme = {
  primary: { bg: "var(--blue-600)", fg: "white" },
  danger:  { bg: "var(--red-600)",  fg: "white" },
} satisfies Theme;  // error if a key is missing or misspelled

type Tone = keyof typeof theme; // "primary" | "danger", still precise
```

Combine with `as const` for config objects, route tables and event name maps where you want both validation and literal types.

## unknown, any and never
- `any` turns off checking in both directions and spreads silently. Avoid it; lint with `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any`.
- `unknown` accepts anything but you must narrow before use. It is the right type for `catch (e)`, `JSON.parse` output and untrusted input.
- `never` is the empty type: a function that never returns, or the leftover after exhaustive narrowing.

## Typing API responses with zod
Types are erased at build time. If the backend renames a field, `as User` happily lies until something crashes far away. Validate at the boundary and infer the type from the schema so there is one source of truth:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const UserSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  name: z.string(),
  email: z.string().email(),
  role: z.enum(["admin", "member"]),
});
export type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;

export async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  return UserSchema.parse(await res.json()); // throws with a precise path on mismatch
}
```

Use `safeParse` when you want to render an error state instead of throwing. The same schema can validate form input on the client and the request body on the server. Alternatives with similar goals are valibot (smaller bundles) and generated types from OpenAPI or GraphQL schemas.

## Common mistakes
- Boolean flag soup instead of a discriminated union.
- `as` assertions to silence errors, especially on fetched data.
- Annotating a config object with a wide type and losing its literal keys (use `satisfies`).
- Returning arrays from hooks without `as const`, which gives a union-element array instead of a tuple.
- `React.FC` with implicit children (removed in React 18 types); just type props directly.

## In the interview
For state modelling, write the discriminated union and show the exhaustive switch with `never`. For generics, write a `List<T>` or `useFetch<T>` and explain inference. Explain `unknown` versus `any` as "safe top type versus opting out". For `satisfies`, give the config-object example. Always mention that TypeScript does not validate runtime data and show zod at the network boundary: interviewers listen for that distinction.

deepDives.body:
A filter click can trigger a URL update, a server render and a large list re-render. The risk is a long task between the click and the next paint.

Approach:

1. **Immediate feedback first**: the checkbox toggles and a subtle pending state appears in the same frame.
2. **Non-urgent update in a transition**: `startTransition(() => router.replace(newUrl))` lets React keep the UI interactive and interrupt the render if the user clicks again.
3. **Keep the DOM small**: render 24 to 48 products per page, or virtualise. Rendering 500 product cards costs more in style and layout than in JavaScript.
4. **Debounce only the expensive inputs** (price slider), not checkboxes.

```tsx
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
function toggle(facet: string, value: string) {
  const params = new URLSearchParams(searchParams);
  params.has(facet, value) ? params.delete(facet, value) : params.append(facet, value);
  startTransition(() => router.replace(`?${params}`, { scroll: false }));
}
```

Measure with `onINP` attribution in the field. If the presentation delay dominates, the DOM update is too big; if the input delay dominates, something else (third-party script, hydration) is blocking the main thread.

A budget only works if breaking it is painful. The project keeps a single budget file and runs Lighthouse CI against a preview deployment on every pull request.

```json
{
  "assertions": {
    "largest-contentful-paint": ["error", { "maxNumericValue": 2500 }],
    "total-blocking-time": ["error", { "maxNumericValue": 200 }],
    "cumulative-layout-shift": ["error", { "maxNumericValue": 0.1 }],
    "resource-summary:script:size": ["error", { "maxNumericValue": 180000 }]
  }
}
```

Lab numbers are noisy, so run three times and assert on the median, and test the three key templates rather than every page. TBT stands in for INP in the lab. Lab budgets catch regressions; field data (p75 from the RUM endpoint, segmented by device) confirms the real experience after deploy, so the dashboard compares the last 7 days to the previous release.

Every local change is written in one IndexedDB transaction to both the `notes` store and an `outbox` store:

```ts
await db.transaction("rw", db.notes, db.outbox, async () => {
  await db.notes.put({ ...note, updatedAt: Date.now() });
  await db.outbox.add({ opId: crypto.randomUUID(), noteId: note.id, op: "upsert", baseVersion: note.version, createdAt: Date.now() });
});
```

Because both writes share a transaction, a crash can never leave a note changed but not queued. The sync engine pushes outbox entries in order. The server stores processed `opId`s, so if the response is lost and the client retries, the op is not applied twice. Successful ops are deleted from the outbox and the note's version is updated from the response.

Pull uses a monotonically increasing server cursor (a change-log sequence number), not timestamps, because client clocks are unreliable. Consecutive edits to the same note can be coalesced in the outbox to reduce traffic.
